### Bug Description

When an AI Agent calls a tool, n8n builds the tool node's input by spreading the **agent's own input item** underneath the arguments the model produced. Behind a Chat Trigger, that item contains `action: "sendMessage"`, `sessionId` and `chatInput`.

The MCP Client Tool then keeps only the keys that appear in the MCP tool's `inputSchema`. So any MCP tool with a parameter named `action` receives `action: "sendMessage"` whenever the model leaves that parameter out. The model never sent it, and the tool node's Input panel does not show it.

I found this with a real MCP server whose `create_signing_session` tool has an optional `action` object parameter. Every call from an n8n agent failed with `path: ["action"], Expected object, received string`. The model correctly stated it had not sent `action`, and the MCP Client node's Input panel (JSON view included) agreed with it.

#### Cause

`packages/core/src/execution-engine/requests-response.ts` (n8n-core 2.47.2, compiled `dist/execution-engine/requests-response.js`):

```js
const agentInputData = executionData.data.main?.[0]?.[itemIndex];
const mergedJson = {
  ...(agentInputData?.json ?? {}),   // the trigger item: action, sessionId, chatInput, ...
  ...action.input,                   // the model's tool arguments
  toolCallId: action.id,
};
const displayJson = { ...action.input };   // what the tool node's Input panel shows
```

`mergedJson` becomes the tool node's input item. In `@n8n/n8n-nodes-langchain` 2.47.3, `nodes/mcp/shared/runtime.ts`, `runToolCall` then does:

```js
const sanitizedToolArguments = schema.additionalProperties !== true
  ? pick(toolArguments, Object.keys(schema.properties ?? {}))
  : toolArguments;
```

So a trigger field survives exactly when its name matches a tool parameter. With `additionalProperties: true`, every trigger field would be sent.

#### Scope

- **Confirmed by observation:** `action` from the Chat Trigger reaching an MCP tool with an `action` parameter.
- **Follows from the code but not observed:** any field of the agent's input item, for example `sessionId`, `chatInput`, or the fields of whatever trigger feeds the agent, colliding with a same-named tool parameter. Other tool types that read parameters from the input item may be affected as well; I only tested the MCP Client Tool.

#### Why it is hard to debug

The Input panel renders `displayJson`, not `mergedJson`. The tool node's input as shown contains no `action`, while the request that went out did. Combined with an error naming a field nobody sent, a user (and the agent itself) will look for the problem everywhere except n8n. I needed server-side logging of argument names to see it.

Related in theme though not the same bug: #36863 reports a different MCP Client Tool failure that is likewise recorded as a successful execution, with a message that does not name its cause.

### To Reproduce

Minimal server: one tool, no validation. `echo(text: string, action?: string)` returns exactly the arguments it received and logs each call.

<details><summary><code>server.mjs</code> (~60 lines, only dependency <code>@modelcontextprotocol/sdk</code>)</summary>

```js
// Minimal MCP server (Streamable HTTP, stateless) with one tool, `echo`,
// that returns EXACTLY the arguments it received. No validation, no zod:
// whatever the client sends is what comes back.
//
//   npm install && node server.mjs          # listens on 0.0.0.0:3333/mcp
//   PORT=4000 node server.mjs
import http from 'node:http';
import { Server } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/index.js';
import { StreamableHTTPServerTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/streamableHttp.js';
import { ListToolsRequestSchema, CallToolRequestSchema } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/types.js';

const PORT = Number(process.env.PORT ?? 3333);

function buildServer() {
  const server = new Server({ name: 'echo-mcp', version: '1.0.0' }, { capabilities: { tools: {} } });

  server.setRequestHandler(ListToolsRequestSchema, async () => ({
    tools: [
      {
        name: 'echo',
        description:
          'Echo back the arguments exactly as received. `text` is required. ' +
          '`action` is optional: only send it if the user explicitly gives an action.',
        inputSchema: {
          type: 'object',
          properties: {
            text: { type: 'string', description: 'Text to echo.' },
            action: { type: 'string', description: 'Optional. Leave out unless the user gives one.' },
          },
          required: ['text'],
          additionalProperties: false,
        },
      },
    ],
  }));

  server.setRequestHandler(CallToolRequestSchema, async (req) => {
    const received = req.params.arguments ?? {};
    console.log(new Date().toISOString(), 'tools/call', req.params.name, JSON.stringify(received));
    return { content: [{ type: 'text', text: JSON.stringify({ received }) }] };
  });

  return server;
}

http
  .createServer(async (req, res) => {
    if (!req.url?.startsWith('/mcp')) { res.writeHead(404).end(); return; }
    if (req.method !== 'POST') { res.writeHead(405, { Allow: 'POST' }).end(); return; }
    let raw = '';
    for await (const chunk of req) raw += chunk;
    let body;
    try { body = JSON.parse(raw); } catch { res.writeHead(400).end('bad json'); return; }

    // Stateless: a fresh server + transport per request.
    const server = buildServer();
    const transport = new StreamableHTTPServerTransport({ sessionIdGenerator: undefined });
    res.on('close', () => { transport.close(); server.close(); });
    await server.connect(transport);
    await transport.handleRequest(req, res, body);
  })
  .listen(PORT, '0.0.0.0', () => console.log(`echo-mcp listening on http://0.0.0.0:${PORT}/mcp`));
```

</details>

1. Save it as `server.mjs`, then:
   ```
   npm init -y && npm pkg set type=module && npm install @modelcontextprotocol/sdk@1.29.0
   node server.mjs        # listens on 0.0.0.0:3333/mcp
   ```
2. Workflow: **When chat message received** -> **AI Agent** (any chat model) with an **MCP Client** tool pointed at `http://<host>:3333/mcp` (HTTP Streamable, no auth, no memory node).
3. Chat: `Call the echo tool with text "hello". Do not pass an action.`

**Expected:** server receives `{"text":"hello"}`.
**Actual:** server receives `{"text":"hello","action":"sendMessage"}`.

Echo server console (n8n 2.47.4, no memory, two separate fresh chat sessions, 2/2 reproduced):

```
2026-11-14T00:31:05.829Z tools/call echo {"text":"hello","action":"sendMessage"}
2026-11-14T00:32:53.059Z tools/call echo {"text":"hello","action":"sendMessage"}
```

MCP Client node, same execution:

- **Input** panel: `text: hello`, `tool: MCP_Client_echo` (no `action`)
- **Output**: `{"received":{"text":"hello","action":"sendMessage"}}`

(`tool: MCP_Client_echo` is n8n's internal dispatch key and is expected. It is only visible here because an echo tool returns everything it is given.)

The agent's own reply: *"The response also included "action": "sendMessage", even though I didn't pass an action. I'm not sure where that value came from."*

Also observed on the original server, which logs argument names and types but never values on failed calls. Two calls whose n8n Input panel (JSON view) contained no `action` arrived as:

```
"action": "string(11)"      // length of "sendMessage"
```

Across retries the model varied its arguments in response to an error naming a field it had not sent, with no way to see the request that was actually issued.

A direct MCP client (`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` `Client` plus `StreamableHTTPClientTransport`) calling the same server with `{text:"hello"}` receives `{"text":"hello"}` back, so the extra field is added inside n8n.

### Expected behavior

Only the arguments the model produced should reach the MCP tool. A trigger field should never be able to collide with a tool's declared parameter.

#### Workaround

Put an **Edit Fields (Set)** node between the Chat Trigger and the AI Agent that keeps only `chatInput` and `sessionId`, with "Include Other Input Fields" off. `action` no longer reaches the agent's input item, so it no longer reaches tools.

Verified with the same workflow, same message and same server, the only change being the Edit Fields node, in a fresh session:

```
2026-11-14T00:50:15.243Z tools/call echo {"text":"hello"}
```

#### Suggested fix

Build the tool's arguments only from `action.input` plus `toolCallId`. If nodes need the parent item for expressions, pass it separately rather than merging it into the same object as the arguments. At minimum, render the merged object in the Input panel so the request that was sent is visible.

Happy to open a PR if you agree on the approach.

